{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>If the sun has risen upon him</b> — this is nothing but a kind of parable, etc. (this is the language of Rashi, as he concludes below). And Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 22:2), who translated \"if the eye of witnesses fell upon him,\" adopted a different approach, to say that if witnesses found him before the master of the house came, and when the master of the house came against him they warned him not to kill him — he has blood-guilt if he killed him; once there are witnesses seeing that the thief did not come for murder and would not kill the master of the house. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 22:2). And I am astonished, for when Scripture said above \"he has no blood-guilt\" to acquit him for killing him, it is surely with warning, for a murderer is never liable to execution except with warning. And if you say that Scripture said \"he has no blood-guilt\" only to permit his blood from Heavenly punishment, to say that it is permitted to kill him — this is not true, but the first verse acquits him in every respect, both from Heaven and from a court with warning, and the second verse makes him liable in both regards. Perhaps the Rav means that if witnesses found him before the master of the house came and identified him, and the thief knew of them, he is no longer coming for murder, for he has already seen that the witnesses identified him, and if he kills, witnesses will come to court and have him executed. And this is the meaning of <b>if the sun has risen upon him</b>, for at night, when the witnesses did not identify him, he might kill the master of the house and flee.\n\nIn my opinion, Onkelos means to say that if the thief emerged from the tunnel, and the master of the house came to testify against him that there are witnesses that he entered the tunnel, he has blood like any other living person, and it is not permitted to kill him, and if he killed him he is executed for it; but he must pay if he took anything from there. And Scripture said \"if the sun has risen upon him,\" speaking in the present tense, for the way of one who tunnels is to come at night, when no one recognizes him, and one who kills there is exempt and it is permitted; but one who lingers there until the sun has risen upon him is lying in wait and emerging and fleeing for his life. And if the master of the house comes to hold him liable on the testimony of witnesses, he is not liable to death, neither by court nor by the hand of the master of the house. And if so, according to the opinion in the Talmud (Sanhedrin 72a) that one who enters the tunnel and took vessels and emerged is exempt — \"in blood he acquired it\" — then <b>he shall surely pay; if he has nothing, he shall be sold for his theft</b> refers back above to \"when a man steals\" (Exodus 21:37), in the manner of \"and likewise you shall do to your maidservant\" (Deuteronomy 15:17).\n\nAnd the way of the peshat is well known: it says that if he tunneled in darkness and was found in the tunnel at night, he is killed; and if the sun has risen upon the thief and a person sees him and recognizes him, he is not killed, but he must pay what he stole and took out from there by day. And the meaning of \"the sun\" is the light before the eyes of the seers, and so too \"before the sun\" (II Samuel 12:11) means in the open. And the reason for this law is as we have mentioned: this one would kill the master of the house, and that one would flee from him."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The Rav (הרב) here appears to refer to Maimonides; the abbreviation ב\"ד throughout is read as בית דין (court)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:18:26.101607808Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}